Find the modular inverse of $7$ modulo $26$,
i.e.\ find $x$ such that $7x \equiv 1 \pmod{26}$.

Solution:

Step 1: Apply Euclidean Algorithm to find $\gcd(7,26)$.
$$\begin{align*}
    26 &= 7(3) + 5 \\
    7  &= 5(1) + 2 \\
    5  &= 2(2) + 1 \\
    2  &= 1(2) + 0
\end{align*}$$
$\gcd(7,26) = 1$ (coprime, so the inverse exists). ✓

Step 2: Back-substitute.
$$\begin{align*}
    1 &= 5 - 2(2) \\
      &= 5 - 2(7 - 5) = 3(5) - 2(7) \\
      &= 3(26 - 7 \cdot 3) - 2(7) = 3(26) - 9(7) - 2(7) \\
      &= 3(26) - 11(7)
\end{align*}$$
So $-11(7) \equiv 1 \pmod{26}$.

Since $-11 \equiv 15 \pmod{26}$:
\[
    \boxed{7^{-1} \equiv 15 \pmod{26}}
\]

Verify: $7 \times 15 = 105 = 4(26) + 1 \equiv 1 \pmod{26}$ ✓

(This modular inverse is used in the Affine Cipher in Section~3.6.)

